# Structs, Enums & Pattern Matching

## Structs model *things*

```rust
struct Task {
    title: String,
    done: bool,
}

impl Task {
    fn new(title: impl Into<String>) -> Self {
        Self { title: title.into(), done: false }
    }
    fn complete(&mut self) {
        self.done = true;
    }
}
```

`&self` reads, `&mut self` mutates, `self` consumes. Constructors are plain
associated functions named `new`.

## Enums model *choices* (sum types)

```rust
enum Shape {
    Circle { radius: f64 },
    Rect { w: f64, h: f64 },
}
```

Each variant can carry data — this replaces class hierarchies.

## `match` must be exhaustive

```rust
fn area(shape: &Shape) -> f64 {
    match shape {
        Shape::Circle { radius } => std::f64::consts::PI * radius * radius,
        Shape::Rect { w, h } => w * h,
    }
}
```

Add a variant later and the compiler lists every match to update — refactoring
with a net. Use `_ =>` sparingly; it silences that safety.

examples.code:
enum Shape {
    Circle { radius: f64 },
    Rect { w: f64, h: f64 },
}

fn area(shape: &Shape) -> f64 {
    match shape {
        Shape::Circle { radius } => std::f64::consts::PI * radius * radius,
        Shape::Rect { w, h } => w * h,
    }
}

fn main() {
    let shapes = [Shape::Circle { radius: 1.0 }, Shape::Rect { w: 2.0, h: 3.0 }];
    for s in &shapes {
        println!("{:.2}", area(s));
    }
}

